;; std/ scale sweep — look for superlinearity in Brood policy code.
;;
;; Each row runs the same work at N and 4N. A linear op reports ~4x; a quadratic
;; reports ~16x. Two of the three quadratics fixed on 2026-07-30 were in std/,
;; not the kernel, and nothing in the suite exercises these frameworks at scale.
;;
;;   N=2000 brood scripts/fuzz/stress/scale_sweep.blsp
;;
;; TWO POINTS ARE NOT A MEASUREMENT — read this before believing a row.
;; A single invocation's two timings are NOT enough to call a shape. Readings for
;; `proc/gen gen-call` on 2026-07-30, same binary (56c2501):
;;
;;   base N=2000 -> ratio 10.21, then 7.77 on a re-run   (flags SUPERLINEAR)
;;   base N=4000 -> ratio  2.93                          (reads linear)
;;
;; And the absolutes disagree ACROSS invocations: 8000 calls cost 143 ms in one
;; process, 16000 cost 217 ms in another. A genuine quadratic does NOT get better
;; as the base grows, so this is not one; the shape is consistent with a fixed
;; cost (GC growth / tier-up) crossing a threshold between 2000 and 8000 calls,
;; which both runs at base 4000 already pay. Do not report it as a quadratic
;; without doing the work below — the ratio column at small N is not evidence.
;;
;; To actually confirm a row: sweep a THIRD point (N, 4N, 16N) inside ONE
;; invocation, repeat the whole invocation an odd number of times, take medians,
;; and establish the row's own base-vs-base floor first (see docs/handoff.md §5).
;; A ratio near 16x that HOLDS or GROWS across three points and repeated runs is
;; a quadratic; anything smaller needs the floor before it is reportable.
;;
;; Rows measured linear on 2026-07-30: proc/agent update, buffer insert,
;; buffer forward-line. proc/gen gen-call is unresolved, per the above.
;;
;; 2026-08-04: `sexp motions` WAS a confirmed quadratic — three rising points, ASCII 5.68x
;; then 9.40x, one-non-ASCII 8.84x then 12.30x. Each motion's cost is `narrow`'s call to the
;; native form-start scan, which is O(point) by design (a backward scan cannot know string
;; state). Two constant-factor fixes landed first (the native stopped copying the whole
;; buffer, -18%; `narrow` stopped making the O(point) pass twice, -39% more) and did NOT
;; change the shape.
;;
;; FIXED 2026-08-04 (ADR-214), and now LINEAR: the scan resumes from a safepoint table
;; cached against the string value, so a motion costs O(stride) instead of O(point). Same
;; profile both sides, base 3200: ratio 7.97 -> 3.88 (ASCII) and 7.87 -> 3.89 (UTF-8), with
;; 12800 forms 18570 -> 6146 ms (3.0x). Linearity confirmed on FOUR rising bases
;; (4.04 / 3.99 / 3.85 / 3.94), which is the non-rising trend this header demands.
;; NOTE the buffer-command path (`sexp/forward` on a buffer) still pays O(n) per motion in
;; `(buffer-text buf)` = `rope->string`, a fresh string the table can never hit; this row
;; measures the shape that holds ONE text value, which is the tooling/LSP shape.
;; Measure the SEQUENCE — one motion looks fine at any buffer size.
;;
;; The multi-byte gap WAS the striking part: 27571 vs 6037 ms at 6400 forms, 4.6x — `substring`
;; being O(index) rather than O(result) off the ASCII fast path. **Gone since ADR-213** (the
;; string slot's sparse char->byte index): the row now reads the SAME ratio in both regimes
;; (5.42 vs 5.31 at base 800, 8.13 multi-byte at base 3200 against 9.40 ASCII previously). What
;; is left is the O(point) scan above, which is encoding-independent by construction.
;;
;; 2026-08-04: `editor/markdown` markdown-spans WAS quadratic on MULTI-BYTE ONLY — linear in
;; ASCII (3.81x/3.88x) and 5.03x then 6.92x rising with one non-ASCII char, because the
;; per-line `(substring src i e)` and newline scan are O(i) off the ASCII fast path. Fixed
;; with one `string-split` + a running offset (the `stream-lines` fix again): 6400 lines
;; 1287 -> 559 ms, and multi-byte now EQUALS ASCII (559 vs 556). Lesson: a row can be linear
;; in ASCII and quadratic in UTF-8, so sweep BOTH — a pure-ASCII corpus hides this entirely.
;;
;; 2026-08-04, cleared: `editor/highlight` highlight-spans is LINEAR even on multi-byte
;; (16x input for 14.6x time; the multi-byte ratio FALLS 4.49 -> 3.42). The
;; char-at-over-whole-text shape is present there and is not quadratic, so that shape by
;; itself is not evidence — measure before believing a grep.
;;
;; 2026-08-02: `template render` WAS a confirmed quadratic — 12.0x then 13.4x across
;; three points, holding and growing, which is the signature the caveat above asks for.
;; Fixed (one native split + one join); now 5.0x / 4.80x, and 318 ms -> 24 ms at N=3200.
;; 2026-08-02: `last-index-of` WAS a confirmed quadratic — 16.5x then 16.4x — because it
;; walked forward in Brood calling `index-of` per match. Now one native pass:
;; 540 ms -> 1 ms at 6400 repeats. It is on an editor hot path both ways (buffer reverse
;; search over whole buffer text; lineedit finding the line start per keystroke).
;;
;; 2026-08-02: `incremental scan` — a Brood loop calling `(index-of s needle i)` with a
;; rising `i` — is now LINEAR too, and it took two fixes, only one of which was the one
;; predicted. (a) the heap string slot caches its CHAR count, so `string-length` is O(1)
;; and `chars == bytes` is an O(1) pure-ASCII test, making char<->byte conversion O(1);
;; (b) the far bigger one, `expect_string` returns an OWNED String — it copied the whole
;; haystack on every call. Borrowing instead (`expect_string_ref`, the sibling of the
;; existing `expect_rope_ref`) is what actually moved it: 1114 ms -> 15 ms at 25 600
;; repeats, ratios 12.33/30.10 -> 7.0/2.14 (a falling ratio is warm-up, i.e. linear).
;; NOTE `expect_string` is used ~120 times across the builtins and every one of those is a
;; per-call copy of its string argument. Only the two search primitives were converted
;; here; the rest is an open seam.
;;
;; CORRECTION 2026-08-04: "is now LINEAR too" above is true ONLY FOR ASCII, and the fix's
;; own mechanism is why — `chars == bytes` *is* the pure-ASCII test, so the O(1) char<->byte
;; conversion it buys exists only on that fast path. Re-measured with `UTF8=1`: inc-scan is
;; 7 -> 118 ms, ratio **16.85** at N=800, a textbook quadratic, while the same row in ASCII
;; is 0/2 ms and unmeasurable. The in-code comment on the row ("Still O(n^2)") was right and
;; the header's claim was over-broad. This is the kernel lever, not a std/ fix: char->byte
;; conversion needs an index for non-ASCII strings.
;;
;; FIXED 2026-08-04 (ADR-213): each string slot builds a sparse char->byte index on its first
;; conversion, so a char index costs a lookup plus a walk bounded by 32 chars in EITHER
;; encoding. inc-scan under `UTF8=1`: 7 -> 118 ms (16.85x) becomes 0 -> 3 ms, and 3 -> 8 ms
;; (2.66x, falling = warm-up) at base 3200. Both string rows are now regime-independent, so
;; this row's job from here is to catch a regression in EITHER regime — keep running it both
;; ways.
;;
;; 2026-08-02: `strip-ansi` WAS a confirmed quadratic — 7.6x then 14.0x, 1.58 s for ~90 KB
;; of colourised output. It read each char with `char-at` (O(i) on UTF-8) AND called
;; `string-length` (`chars().count()`, O(n)) per character: two stacked quadratics. Now a
;; code-point vector with a threaded length, like `url.blsp` / `csv.blsp`. 1583 ms -> 109 ms
;; at 3200 lines. NOTE it has no in-tree callers — a latent bug in a public std module.
;;
;; 2026-08-04: `sse frames-1c` WAS a confirmed quadratic — 8.25x / 20.33x / 18.47x across
;; three rising bases — the `stream-lines` bug in another module: `sse--frames` re-`substring`ed
;; the REST of the buffer per event, so ONE socket read holding k events cost O(k * chunk), and
;; a 64 KB read of short events is the normal case. Now one native `string-split` whose last
;; piece IS the remainder: 25600 events 5671 -> 15 ms, ratio 5.0 at the top base.
;;
;; 2026-08-02: `stream-lines` WAS quadratic INSIDE a chunk — it re-`substring`ed the rest of
;; the buffer per line. The prediction that it is bounded by chunk size held exactly: a
;; 64-line drip measured 4.50/4.27 (linear) while one chunk holding k lines measured
;; 11.50/13.17. That matters because one big chunk is the normal case — a 64 KB socket read
;; is ~1700 short lines. Now one `string-split` per chunk with the complete lines queued:
;; 303 ms -> 39 ms at 3200 lines in one chunk.
;;
;; Its row reads ~5-8x at small N and 1.82x from 3200->12800 — a ratio that FALLS as the
;; base grows is warm-up, not a quadratic (the same signature that cleared `proc/gen
;; gen-call`). 800 -> 12800 is 5 -> 71 ms, i.e. 14x for 16x of input.
;;
;; `format format-source` was cleared as LINEAR on 3.80x / 4.12x / 4.64x and that call was
;; WRONG — a cautionary row. Those ratios creep UP, and pushing the base further gave 4.46
;; then 6.40, with 12 800 forms taking 3.6 s: `format-cst-root--walk` really was recopying
;; the whole output per top-level form, exactly as its `(str acc …)` shape suggested. The
;; per-form render dominates at small N and hides it. Fixed 2026-08-02 (reversed chunk list,
;; joined once): 3593 ms -> 1988 ms at 12 800, ratios 3.79 / 4.22, and 4.26 / 4.16 at the
;; next base up. LESSON: a ratio near 4 that RISES across bases is not linear — only a
;; falling one (warm-up) clears a row. Check the trend, not one triple.

;; `:only` on the two proc modules: they both export `stop` (an import clash the
;; namespacing hardening now rejects outright), and `proc/agent` also shadows the prelude's
;; `get`/`update`. `gen-calls` below already calls `proc/gen/stop` qualified.
(defmodule scale-sweep
  ;; `sse--frames` is module-private (ADR-146); a stress harness measuring an internal is
  ;; exactly the `:use-internals` case, as `tests/sse_test.blsp` does.
  (:use-internals net/sse)
  (:use proc/agent :only [start update stop])
  (:use proc/gen :only [defprocess spawn-server gen-call])
  (:use editor/buffer)
  (:use sexp :only [point-forward]))
(require 'template)
(require 'format)
(require 'ansi)
(require 'stream)
(require 'net/sse)

(def n (let (e (getenv "N")) (if e (string->number e) 2000)))

;; UTF8=1 re-runs every string row with a NON-ASCII lead, which is a different complexity
;; regime, not a cosmetic variation: a char index is a byte offset only for pure-ASCII text,
;; so every char->byte conversion is O(1) on ASCII and O(index) off it. `editor/markdown` was
;; linear on ASCII (3.81x/3.88x) and 5.03x/6.92x rising with ONE non-ASCII character
;; (2026-08-04). Every row here was originally cleared on ASCII fixtures, so a row cleared
;; without this knob is only half-cleared. One lead char is enough — it is the string's
;; ASCII-ness that is lost, not the position of the character.
(def utf8? (not (nil? (getenv "UTF8"))))
(def utf8-lead (if utf8? "café — " ""))
;; Source-shaped fixtures must stay parseable, so lead them with a comment instead.
(def utf8-src-lead (if utf8? ";; café — em dash\n" ""))

(defn ms (f)
  (let (t0 (now-ns)
        _ (f))
    (quot (- (now-ns) t0) 1000000)))

(defn us (f k)
  (let (t0 (now-ns)
        _ (f))
    (quot (- (now-ns) t0) (* k 1000))))

(defn row (label base f)
  (let (t1 (ms (fn () (f base)))
        t4 (ms (fn () (f (* 4 base))))
        ratio (if (= t1 0) 0 (quot (* 100 t4) t1)))
    (println (str label
               " N="
               base
               ": "
               t1
               " ms   4N: "
               t4
               " ms   ratio "
               (quot ratio 100)
               "."
               (rem ratio 100)
               (if (> ratio 800) "   <-- SUPERLINEAR" "")))))

;; ---- proc/agent: N sequential updates against one agent ---------------------

(defn loop-updates (pid k)
  (if (= k 0)
    nil
    (do
      (update pid (fn (s) (+ s 1)))
      (loop-updates pid (- k 1)))))

(defn agent-updates (k)
  (let (pid (start (fn () 0))
        _ (loop-updates pid k))
    (stop pid)))

;; ---- proc/gen: N synchronous calls against one server -----------------------

(defprocess counter-server
  (state)
  (call :bump [(+ state 1) (+ state 1)]))

(defn loop-calls (pid k)
  (if (= k 0)
    nil
    (do
      (gen-call pid :bump)
      (loop-calls pid (- k 1)))))

(defn gen-calls (k)
  (let (pid (spawn-server counter-server 0)
        _ (loop-calls pid k))
    (proc/gen/stop pid)))

;; ---- editor/buffer: N inserts, then N line navigations ----------------------

(defn loop-inserts (buf k)
  (if (= k 0)
    buf
    (loop-inserts (insert buf "hello\n") (- k 1))))

(defn buffer-inserts (k)
  (loop-inserts (make-buffer "") k))

(defn loop-nav (buf k)
  (if (= k 0)
    buf
    (loop-nav (forward-line buf 1) (- k 1))))

(defn buffer-nav (k)
  (let (buf (loop-inserts (make-buffer "") k))
    (loop-nav (goto-char buf 0) k)))

;; ---- template: N markers substituted into one template ----------------------
;; Was O(n^2): the scan re-sliced the REST of the template per marker AND re-`str`'d the
;; whole output so far. Both are gone; keep the row so it cannot come back.

(defn tmpl-of (k) (str utf8-lead (apply str (repeat k "xxxxxxxxxx {{a}} "))))
(defn tmpl-render (k) (template/render (tmpl-of k) {"a" "VALUE"}))

;; ---- format: N small top-level forms through the formatter ------------------

(defn src-of (k)
  (str utf8-src-lead (apply str (repeat k "(defn f (a b) (if (> a b) (+ a 1) (- b 1)))\n\n"))))
(defn fmt-source (k) (format/format-source (src-of k)))

;; ---- ansi: strip escapes from colourised subprocess output ------------------

(defn colored-of (k)
  (str utf8-lead (apply str (repeat k "\e[32mok\e[0m  some test name here\r\n"))))
(defn ansi-strip (k) (ansi/strip-ansi (colored-of k)))

;; ---- stream-lines: k lines arriving in ONE chunk ----------------------------
;; The drip case (many small chunks) was always linear; this is the shape that was not.

(defn stream-lines-1chunk (k)
  (let (blob (str utf8-lead (apply str (repeat k "some log line of moderate length here\n"))))
    (count (stream/stream-to-list (stream/stream-lines (stream/stream-from-list (list blob)))))))

;; ---- SSE frame extraction from one accumulated chunk ------------------------

;; The `stream-lines` shape in another module: `sse--frames` used to `substring` the REST of
;; the buffer per event, so ONE socket read holding k events cost O(k * chunk). A 64 KB read
;; of short events is the normal case, which is why this is measured with all k events in a
;; single buffer rather than dripped.
(defn sse-frames-1chunk (k)
  (let (blob (str utf8-lead (apply str (repeat k "event: tick\ndata: some payload here\n\n"))))
    (count (first (net/sse/sse--frames blob)))))

;; ---- string search: reverse, and a forward incremental scan -----------------

(defn doc-of (k)
  (str utf8-lead (apply str (repeat k "lorem ipsum dolor NEEDLE sit amet consectetur "))))
(defn scan-all (s needle i n)
  (let (j (index-of s needle i)) (if (< j 0) n (scan-all s needle (inc j) (inc n)))))
;; Linear in BOTH encoding regimes since ADR-213 (was 16.85x under UTF8=1, and effectively
;; free in ASCII the whole time — see the header's correction and its FIXED note).
(defn inc-scan (k) (let (d (doc-of k)) (scan-all d "NEEDLE" 0 0)))
(defn last-idx (k) (let (d (doc-of k)) (last-index-of d "NEEDLE")))

;; ---- run -------------------------------------------------------------------

(println "std/ scale sweep — linear ~4x, quadratic ~16x")
(def sweep--src-line
  "(defn compute-thing (alpha beta) (if (< alpha beta) (+ alpha 1) (str \"x\" beta)))\n")
(defn sweep--src (k acc)
  (if (= k 0) (cons utf8-src-lead acc) (sweep--src (- k 1) (cons sweep--src-line acc))))
;; A SEQUENCE of structural motions across one buffer — the editor's repeated
;; `forward-sexp`, not one motion. Each motion calls `sexp/narrow`, which calls the native
;; `scan-form-start` TWICE, and that is a forward lexical pass from offset 0 by design (a
;; backward scan cannot know whether a bracket sits inside a string). So per motion is
;; O(point) and the sequence is O(n^2). Confirmed 2026-08-04 on three rising points:
;; ASCII 5.68x then 9.40x; with ONE non-ASCII char 8.84x then 12.30x. Not fixed — the
;; asymptote needs cached lexer state or a bounded safe-restart, which the pure
;; `(text point) -> point` API forecloses. See docs/handoff.md.
(defn sexp-motions (k)
  (let (text (join "" (sweep--src k nil)))
    (fold (fn (p _) (let (np (point-forward text p)) (if (or (nil? np) (= np p)) p np)))
      0
      (range 0 k))))

(row "proc/agent  update  " n agent-updates)
(row "proc/gen    gen-call" n gen-calls)
(row "buffer      insert  " n buffer-inserts)
(row "buffer      fwd-line" n buffer-nav)
(row "template    render  " n tmpl-render)
(row "format      fmt-src " n fmt-source)
(row "ansi        strip   " n ansi-strip)
(row "stream      lines-1c" n stream-lines-1chunk)
(row "sse         frames-1c" n sse-frames-1chunk)
(row "string      last-idx" n last-idx)
(row "string      inc-scan" n inc-scan) ; was multi-byte-quadratic, fixed — see header
(row "sexp        motions " n sexp-motions) ; was quadratic, fixed (ADR-214) — see header
